 This is a basic problem in number theory. We are given that when Ray climbs the stairs \(4\) steps at a time, there are \(3\) steps left, and when he climbs \(5\) steps at a time, there are \(2\) steps left. We need to find the smallest possible value of \(n\) greater than \(10\) that satisfies both conditions. We can set up the following system of congruences: \[
\begin{cases}
n \equiv 3 \pmod{4} \\
n \equiv 2 \pmod{5}
\end{cases}
\]
We can solve this system step by step. First, we express \(n\) from the second congruence: \(n = 5k + 2\) for some integer \(k\). Substituting this into the first congruence, we get \(5k + 2 \equiv 3 \pmod{4}\). Simplifying, we find \(5k \equiv 1 \pmod{4}\). Since \(5 \equiv 1 \pmod{4}\), this simplifies further to \(k \equiv 1 \pmod{4}\), so \(k = 4m + 1\) for some integer \(m\). Substituting back, we get \(n = 5(4m + 1) + 2 = 20m + 7\). We need \(n > 10\), so the smallest possible value for \(n\) is \(27\) (when \(m = 1\)). </think> <answer>27</answer>
